1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,307
This is InvoiceFlow, an enterprise invoice payment and reconciliation application for the SC6113 individual assignment.

2
00:00:07,307 --> 00:00:18,025
Small businesses need to know which customer paid which invoice, whether the amount is correct, and whether the payment is actually confirmed.

3
00:00:18,025 --> 00:00:24,846
InvoiceFlow connects those business questions to a smart contract and an independently verified ledger.

4
00:00:24,846 --> 00:00:31,666
This demonstration uses a local Ethereum compatible blockchain, public development wallets, and test Ether.

5
00:00:31,666 --> 00:00:38,000
It does not claim a live Sepolia deployment or a tested MetaMask extension.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,700
The interface is built with HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and ethers version six.

7
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:51,350
The Solidity contract stores the issuer, customer, amount, due time, status, and document hash.

8
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:59,900
Flask serves the interface, authenticates issuer signatures, stores invoice descriptions in SQLite, and reads the blockchain for reconciliation.

9
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:03,700
The browser signs transactions through a wallet provider.

10
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:12,250
The backend never holds a customer private key and never treats a client supplied paid flag as proof.

11
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:17,000
The network and contract address are visible in the workspace.

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,452
I select the merchant development wallet and create a new invoice.

13
00:01:22,452 --> 00:01:26,095
The reference identifies the invoice within this merchant's account.

14
00:01:26,095 --> 00:01:31,357
Another merchant may use the same reference, but this merchant cannot reuse it.

15
00:01:31,357 --> 00:01:37,833
I enter a consulting description, the customer's wallet, an Ether amount, and a future due time.

16
00:01:37,833 --> 00:01:41,476
The merchant wallet is taken from the transaction sender.

17
00:01:41,476 --> 00:01:46,738
The fixed customer wallet prevents an unrelated account from claiming this invoice payment.

18
00:01:46,738 --> 00:01:51,999
Descriptions remain off chain, while their content commitment is stored in the contract.

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,385
First I enter a zero amount.

20
00:01:55,385 --> 00:02:01,746
The interface rejects it before a transaction is submitted and explains how to correct the input.

21
00:02:01,746 --> 00:02:05,325
This is a usability check, not the security boundary.

22
00:02:05,325 --> 00:02:16,457
A caller can bypass a web form, so the smart contract also rejects a zero amount, a zero address, self payment, an invalid due time, or empty hashes.

23
00:02:16,457 --> 00:02:21,228
Monetary values are converted to integer wei rather than floating point numbers.

24
00:02:21,228 --> 00:02:26,000
I now correct the amount to zero point zero one five Ether.

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,349
Creating the invoice requires an issuer login signature and a blockchain transaction.

26
00:02:32,349 --> 00:02:39,481
The authentication message includes the origin, chain, a single use nonce, and a five minute expiry.

27
00:02:39,481 --> 00:02:43,493
It authorizes metadata editing, not a transfer of funds.

28
00:02:43,493 --> 00:02:52,409
After the receipt succeeds, Flask verifies that the signed issuer and canonical metadata match the chain before storing the description.

29
00:02:52,409 --> 00:02:55,530
The ledger displays a verified integrity indicator.

30
00:02:55,530 --> 00:03:04,000
If upload fails after creation, the browser keeps a recovery copy rather than pretending the chain transaction was undone.

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,073
I switch to the customer development wallet.

32
00:03:08,073 --> 00:03:13,341
The application shows the payment action for invoices payable by this account.

33
00:03:13,341 --> 00:03:15,536
I pay the selected invoice.

34
00:03:15,536 --> 00:03:22,121
The contract checks that it is open, not expired, and called by the designated customer.

35
00:03:22,121 --> 00:03:26,073
The transferred value must equal the invoice amount exactly.

36
00:03:26,073 --> 00:03:28,707
Underpayment, overpayment, and repeated payment revert.

37
00:03:28,707 --> 00:03:33,536
On success, the invoice becomes paid and the merchant's credit increases.

38
00:03:33,536 --> 00:03:41,000
These are actual transactions on the local virtual machine, not simulated paid labels in the web interface.

39
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,694
Next I run reconciliation.

40
00:03:43,694 --> 00:03:51,317
Flask reads events from the configured contract and compares the payment with the stored invoice amount and merchant.

41
00:03:51,317 --> 00:03:59,364
Events are unique by network scope, transaction hash, and log index, so running reconciliation twice does not duplicate them.

42
00:03:59,364 --> 00:04:04,023
The local policy requires one confirmation; the Sepolia configuration requires three.

43
00:04:04,023 --> 00:04:07,835
A payment below the threshold is shown as confirming.

44
00:04:07,835 --> 00:04:18,000
The server pins its reads to one block and detects checkpoint hash changes so an indexed payment can be removed after a chain rollback.

45
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,887
Payment settlement creates a withdrawable merchant credit.

46
00:04:21,887 --> 00:04:25,599
I return to the merchant wallet and withdraw it.

47
00:04:25,599 --> 00:04:33,024
The contract checks the credit, clears it, reduces total liability, and only then transfers Ether to the merchant.

48
00:04:33,024 --> 00:04:35,500
A reentrancy lock provides another guard.

49
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:41,687
If the receiving contract rejects Ether, the transaction reverts and the merchant's credit is preserved.

50
00:04:41,687 --> 00:04:45,399
The administrator has no function to divert these funds.

51
00:04:45,399 --> 00:04:52,000
The payment history remains visible after withdrawal because withdrawing revenue does not undo the paid invoice.

52
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,675
I also create a second invoice for a cancelled service request.

53
00:04:57,675 --> 00:05:01,925
The issuing merchant can cancel it while it is open.

54
00:05:01,925 --> 00:05:09,150
After cancellation, a customer cannot pay it and the original reference cannot be reused by that merchant.

55
00:05:09,150 --> 00:05:13,400
Paid invoices cannot be cancelled under this prototype's business rules.

56
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:19,350
Overdue status is computed from blockchain time and payments after the due date fail.

57
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:27,000
This project deliberately excludes refunds, invoice amendments, tax calculations, and disputes, so these rules stay explicit and testable.

58
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:36,727
The evidence includes twenty nine passing contract tests, nineteen backend integration tests, and thirteen browser checks.

59
00:05:36,727 --> 00:05:49,818
Contract tests include an actual reverted payment receipt, failed withdrawal rollback, a malicious reentrant receiver, and a liability invariant across twelve multi merchant payments.

60
00:05:49,818 --> 00:05:59,636
Backend tests cover signature replay, expiry, unauthorized metadata changes, tampering, confirmation thresholds, chain rollback, and safe CSV export.

61
00:05:59,636 --> 00:06:07,272
Browser checks execute invoice creation, payment, withdrawal, cancellation, filters, and a mobile overflow check.

62
00:06:07,272 --> 00:06:16,000
The tested wallet rejection is an injected provider fixture, not a claim about the MetaMask extension.

63
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:27,611
The reconciliation CSV links invoice references to merchant and customer wallets, Ether amounts, settlement status, payment hashes, confirmation counts, and integrity results.

64
00:06:27,611 --> 00:06:34,364
This makes the prototype useful for accounts receivable review rather than just transferring cryptocurrency.

65
00:06:34,364 --> 00:06:40,152
Merchant supplied references that look like spreadsheet formulas are escaped during export.

66
00:06:40,152 --> 00:06:44,011
The responsive interface supports desktop and mobile layouts.

67
00:06:44,011 --> 00:06:58,000
The history identifies payment and withdrawal as separate events, and the details view exposes the immutable document hash so a reviewer can follow the record back to its contract.

68
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:05,702
InvoiceFlow demonstrates a complete invoice settlement and reconciliation workflow, but it is a coursework prototype.

69
00:07:05,702 --> 00:07:14,191
Ether prices fluctuate, the public chain reveals wallet relationships, and hashing low entropy invoice data does not guarantee privacy.

70
00:07:14,191 --> 00:07:20,893
The backend uses a single process SQLite index and is intended for a small dataset.

71
00:07:20,893 --> 00:07:26,255
It is not a production accounting system or an audited payment service.

72
00:07:26,255 --> 00:07:36,978
The remaining deployment step is to use dedicated test wallets on Sepolia, verify the actual MetaMask dialogs, and save those transaction hashes and screenshots.

73
00:07:36,978 --> 00:07:40,999
Thank you for reviewing the implementation and its evidence.
